WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.040
So, who's my guest this week?

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.240
It's the Chief Executive

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.543
of the International Rescue
Committee, David Miliband.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.120
Nearly 80 million people

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.520
were forced to flee their homes last year

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.310
and that was before Russia invaded Ukraine

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.400
fueling a growing global food crisis.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.150
Founded at the request of
Albert Einstein in the 1930s,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.770
the International Rescue Committee

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.070
helps resettle and educate refugees,

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.170
treading a path between policy,

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.960
politics, populism, and people,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.420
not always a conducive mix.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.250
It's President and CEO,

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.790
also knows both government
and non-governmental power

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.370
as a former British Foreign Secretary.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.760
From Ukraine, Syria,
Afghanistan, and South Sudan

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.520
to the U.S. border and
the English Channel.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.510
How can NGOs act in the
best interests of refugees

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.543
while navigating governments
with competing priorities?

00:00:55.906 --> 00:00:57.270
Here's what's coming up?

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.480
Is it frustrating the lack of
awareness and understanding

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.400
people have about displaced
people around the world as well?

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.600
Well, I think that the scenes from Ukraine

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.560
they've created in everyone

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.750
that visceral sense it could be me.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.890
That's a fundamental
mind shift for people.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.690
Do you wanna get back into politics?

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.630
I'm running a $1.4 billion NGO.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.750
We are in the middle of
the largest refugee crisis

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.910
since the Second World War

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:26.973
and I feel genuinely lucky
to be doing this job.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.120
David, thank you very much for joining me.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.020
Thank you for having me.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.460
I thought we'd start with, of course,

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.830
what you focus on which
is displaced people

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.540
and just looking at the numbers,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.580
80 million displaced people,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.520
now Ukraine plus a global food crisis.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:55.830
We've hit 100 million displaced people

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.500
according to the UNRC this year.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.450
And I wondered from your vantage point,

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.250
what are you most concerned about

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.930
with those sorts of numbers?

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.140
So I run an international
humanitarian organization,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.310
the International Rescue Committee

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.630
with this extraordinary heritage.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.640
It was founded by Albert Einstein

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.500
to rescue people from Europe.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.450
Today, we work in 35 countries
that are either in conflict.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.290
So places like Somalia
or parts of Ethiopia,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.080
parts of Syria or places that are hosting

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.120
what you call rightly, the displaced.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.560
And just to be absolutely clear,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.530
these are people who are
forced to flee their homes

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.670
by violence and by
persecution and by disaster.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.890
So, we're not talking about
economic migration here,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:38.723
we're talking about people

00:02:38.723 --> 00:02:41.910
who are victims of conflict and disaster

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:44.670
and the places that we
are most worried about

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:47.340
are those either that
are in the front line.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.120
So, the East of Ukraine
is a classic example,

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.920
parts of Ethiopia today.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.660
There's fortunately a cease fire in Yemen.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:58.650
So, there's less immediate
threat to life and limb there,

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.690
the Afghan conflict is over,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.970
but the places where there
isn't a raging conflict

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.830
but have recently been in conflict,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:08.040
there's frankly not just
food insecurity, hunger,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.530
there's the real thread of mass famine.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.660
So, the Horn of Africa,

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.520
the Sahel region of Africa,

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.920
Afghanistan where 97% of the population,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:17.940
it seems almost incredible,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.960
but these are official UN
figures, are living in poverty.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.670
Yemen - 17 million people dependent on

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.170
World Food Programme
essentially for support.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:30.240
I'm worried that literally we're facing

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.850
a life and death situation
in conflict zones,

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:36.330
but also in places that aren't in conflict

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.670
but where the combination

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.060
of the after effects
of conflict on farming,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.550
the impact of the rise in food prices,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.130
the impact of the rise in energy prices

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.980
means that people are really on the brink

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.110
even before you begin to think about

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.860
humanitarian for health, for
education, for livelihoods.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.390
Because I suppose you are right

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.310
that people's minds stay focused

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:00.990
at the point of conflict, don't they?

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.820
And then they don't know very much perhaps

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.437
about what happens next
and those points where--

00:04:05.437 --> 00:04:08.910
Well, there are 53
different civil conflicts

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.380
going on around the world at the moment.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.400
53, right?

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:14.430
And the diplomacy doesn't work

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.650
because we know diplomacy

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.310
as sorting out relations between states.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.130
These are 53 conflicts within states

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.490
and that's where the displacement
is coming from these days.

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:30.210
And in post-conflict
societies like Afghanistan,

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.610
the danger is that conflict returns.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:35.010
And so we are dealing with a cycle now

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:36.870
that we can identify the problem,

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.550
it's much harder to get to the solutions,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.590
but what we try and do as an NGO,

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.100
as a non-governmental
organization is focus on

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:46.080
helping people survive, recover,

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.610
and gain control of their lives.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.460
I mean, just on what's
happened with Ukraine

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.280
and where attention has been focused.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.840
Do you feel or what's that like

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.910
when you're looking
across the whole picture

00:04:56.910 --> 00:04:58.800
with your teams around the world

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.920
to see how much attention
then suddenly goes elsewhere,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.880
and there were concerns,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.440
and I wanted to get your take on this,

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.800
that perhaps with Ukraine, more sympathy,

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.580
more attention went there
because as some put it,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.770
those people certainly
in the West felt like

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.050
they were relatable, they were like us.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.290
Yes, I think it's
undoubtedly the case that

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:23.310
a war on the continent of
Europe brings out a response,

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.303
especially among Europeans

00:05:24.303 --> 00:05:27.540
that is visceral in a
way that is different

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.990
than if it's in Afghanistan
or Syria or further away.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.510
And I think there are two
aspects of the response

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.340
to the Ukraine crisis
that are very striking.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.370
One, which you were hinting at is

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.970
isn't Ukraine better funded
than everywhere else?

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:43.813
And the short answer to that is yes.

00:05:43.813 --> 00:05:44.646
Is it?

00:05:44.646 --> 00:05:48.330
Yes, it is four, five, six,
seven times as much per head

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.000
as in Syria, Afghanistan, Yemen.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.000
And I think that it's really
important to try and say

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.950
that Ukraine should set a standard

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.500
for the level of help
that people should have

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.280
if they are the victims of
conflict and are on the run.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:07.500
And that help isn't just financial help.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.660
The European Union
pledged to every Ukrainian

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.190
three years residency,

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.110
three years right to work,

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.930
three years access to
education in schools,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.750
that's not available to most
refugees in most places.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:21.420
Now, the second thing
that's different is that

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.010
in the Ukraine crisis,

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.470
refugees have fled to a
rich part of the world.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.620
I mean, even Poland

00:06:28.238 --> 00:06:29.430
is one of the poorer countries in Europe,

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.590
but it is rich by global standards,

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.240
most refugees and displaced people

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.270
in the 100 million figure that
has been quoted by the UN,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.280
most of them are in poor
countries not in rich countries.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.080
They're more likely to be in Uganda,

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.600
Ethiopia, Lebanon, Bangladesh

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.900
than they are in Poland, the UK or the US.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.040
So it places strain

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.170
where there's already strain
in a system as opposed to--

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:53.640
Yes and it's also a site of responsibility

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.590
that is not being fulfilled.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.203
So, essentially the
myth in richer countries

00:06:58.203 --> 00:07:01.050
that they're bearing the
greatest burden isn't true.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.330
And the fact that it's poorer countries

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.940
that are being expected to
bear this responsibility

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.550
is not leading to the kind
of responsibility sharing

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.970
that people like me would argue for.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.090
So on that argument that you make,

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.740
beyond, I suppose, your funding

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.720
and where the money that you get goes,

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:17.520
and you talk about it not being handouts,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.530
trying to be an empowering view of this,

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.080
with those facts of the
majority of those people

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.970
go to poorer countries,

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.370
what isn't being done that should be done?

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.120
Well, look, 3% of the
global humanitarian budget

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.140
goes on education,

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:34.200
but half of the displaced are children.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:37.590
So, swathes of displaced people

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.150
who are refugees or internally displaced

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:40.950
are getting no education at all.

00:07:40.950 --> 00:07:42.900
So, is that about
allocating the money better

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.319
that already exists in
the system or boost it?

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:46.590
You've gotta grow the funds.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:50.670
UN runs appeals for countries in crisis,

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.490
and they are 20% funded, 25% funded,

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.350
so there's significant underfunding

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:56.760
even though the funding has doubled

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.980
more or less in the last decade.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:07:59.970
Why is there underfunding?

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:02.190
Because the needs have
trebled in the last decade.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.830
Essentially the number of
displaced people has trebled.

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.600
The amount of funding has doubled

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.270
and it started from a
point that was too low.

00:08:09.270 --> 00:08:12.775
So, education is massively under-invested.

00:08:12.775 --> 00:08:16.920
60% of maternal deaths in childbirth

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.660
are in fragile and conflict states

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:19.980
from which people are fleeing.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:23.520
And 45% of kids who die
in the first month of life

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:25.440
are in fragile and conflict states.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.850
So there's massive health
needs that are not being met.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:30.840
Nevermind the fact

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.770
that only 3% of the world's
refugees go home every year,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:37.110
end up going home, 3%.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.990
So, there aren't sustainable solutions.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:41.460
Most refugees are not in camps,

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.610
but those that are end up
spending 10, 20, 30 years there,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.260
even though it was set
up as a temporary camp.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:47.400
Those that are in urban areas,

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.980
60% of refugees are in urban areas.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:52.230
They have the opportunities
that exist in urban areas,

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:53.100
economic opportunities,

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.020
but also all the risks
of child trafficking

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:55.920
and the rest of it.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:57.600
So, is that now if you're talking about,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.520
especially just 'cause it
all goes back to funding,

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.630
I mean, it's not all that,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:02.670
but it helps everything else go.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:05.310
Are you then saying particular countries

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:08.280
have to put their hand in
their pocket a lot more

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.260
or are you talking about, I don't know

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.630
a completely different way
of doing this and funding it?

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:14.100
Well, I think that there's no question

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.780
that the richer countries,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.700
I mean, we're talking in London,

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.060
the UK's cut its overseas aid budget

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.280
despite the fact that needs are growing

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.370
and there's only two or
three countries in the world

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:27.780
that meet the UN's target

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.870
that 0.7% of national
income from richer countries

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.270
should go towards development aid

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:35.580
and that development aid
needs increasingly to be

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:36.960
on fragile and conflict states

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.990
because now more or less half
of the world's extreme poor

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.760
are not in the Indias
and Chinas of this world,

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.590
they're in, I mean there
are more extreme poor

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.260
in Nigeria than there are in India,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:47.880
even though the population of India

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.770
is four times that of Nigeria.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:52.110
And the reason is that conflict

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:55.353
is the great driver of
extreme poverty today.

00:09:55.353 --> 00:09:57.600
When you combine conflict with COVID

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.370
and with the climate crisis,

00:09:59.370 --> 00:10:01.320
that's how you get the kind of storm

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.080
that pushes people into
the famine conditions

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:04.913
that we were talking about

00:10:04.913 --> 00:10:05.940
at the beginning of this conversation.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:08.310
If you got all of the rich countries

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:10.183
to get to that level, to the 0.7%,

00:10:10.183 --> 00:10:13.740
I mean the British governments
talked about restoring that

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.390
and it was COVID and it was temporary

00:10:15.390 --> 00:10:17.490
but putting aside if you like one country,

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.140
if you've got all of the
countries to do that,

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.640
would it tally up?

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:23.220
Well, it would tally up
in treating the symptoms.

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.010
But when I tell you there are
53 conflicts in the world,

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:29.130
the only way to really
address the root causes

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:31.230
is to tackle the roots of conflict.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:33.240
I mean, the reason people are fleeing

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.490
is that conflict is untamed

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:37.560
and it's untamed because

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.350
the world doesn't have
good diplomatic tools

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.330
for tackling conflicts within states,

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.577
it's got much better,
not withstanding Ukraine.

00:10:44.577 --> 00:10:46.290
But is that because of a worldview that,

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:47.250
sorry to cut across,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.310
but because there's this idea
that countries should be left

00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:52.920
to deal with their own internal issues.

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.560
There's a significant part of the issue

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:56.970
is the sovereignty argument.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.560
That what goes on within a state

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.900
is only the business of that state.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.170
So, if we choose to bomb our
own citizens as some states do,

00:11:04.170 --> 00:11:06.540
if we choose to deny aid to our citizens,

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:09.330
that's only our business.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:10.350
Second part of it though

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.630
is in a weird way the opposite.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.610
So our analysis is that sovereignty

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:15.900
is being used as a shield

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:18.060
against accountability by governments.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:20.400
But secondly, there's a massive growth

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:21.300
in the number of what are called

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:23.010
internationalized conflicts.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.350
If you look at Libya or you look at Syria

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:29.190
or you look at even Ethiopia,

00:11:29.190 --> 00:11:31.650
there's external interference,

00:11:31.650 --> 00:11:34.920
six, ten, twelve different
countries, Yemen,

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.350
12 different countries with a,

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.630
involved with proxies or other support.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:40.650
So, on the one hand,

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.760
it's the sovereignty argument

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.440
used to keep the international system out

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.940
to keep UN officials out.

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.530
On the other hand,

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.480
internationalization of conflict

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.270
is driving the kind of
impunity that we are seeing

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:52.710
that's driving more people to flee.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:54.570
We've got no appetite in some ways

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:55.650
at the same time, do we?

00:11:55.650 --> 00:11:56.580
For intervention.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.220
I know what you just said
about the outside forces,

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:01.350
but there's this kind
of thing coming together

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:02.183
at the same time

00:12:02.183 --> 00:12:03.210
and you've been a foreign secretary

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:05.040
for this country, for the UK.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.740
And you would've seen how
it works at a state level.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.500
So, how do you deal with
that in a climate of,

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.110
especially, I mean, you even
see it, of course America.

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.300
I was talking to John Bolton,

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:17.220
the former security advisor to Trump

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:18.660
on this program only a few weeks ago.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:23.040
And he says, Biden is just not
doing enough to arm Ukraine,

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:24.270
even at arm's length,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.640
there is a concern because it's doing,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.780
we don't wanna get involved.

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:29.010
So I know you're talking about diplomacy

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:29.850
as opposed to arming,

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:31.230
but there is an appetite,

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.190
there's a lack of appetite

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.720
for getting involved, isn't there?

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:35.127
Well, I think there is,
I think you are right

00:12:35.127 --> 00:12:37.260
and that partly explains

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:40.890
the weakness of the multilateral system.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:45.270
But I would argue that if
these problems aren't tackled,

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.250
you end up with more
political instability,

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:49.950
that in a connected world
ends up rebounding on you.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:51.420
But how do you change it?

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:52.740
Well, I think you have to argue for it.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:53.940
You have to win the political arguments.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:57.570
But why is somebody like Joe
Biden not winning it then?

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:00.510
He said we're back, and is he back?

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:01.343
Well I think he's back

00:13:01.343 --> 00:13:04.080
in some aspects of the
international system,

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.400
but in others he's blocked

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.540
and there are two blockages,

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:07.920
one blockage is public opinion,

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:09.960
which doesn't want him
to be spending his time

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.430
on foreign matters.

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:14.310
Second on something like COVID
where he promised leadership,

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.440
Congress has denied him a penny

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:17.820
of extra international funding.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.610
So he's got one hand tied behind his back,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:22.590
which in a way reflects
the domestic politics.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.570
Look, my perspective on this is that

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:28.260
the West is in retreat for some reasons

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.140
which are understandable to
do with domestic pressure

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:32.880
and there's vacuum in a lot of places.

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:35.460
That's the vacuum that
tempted President Putin

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.050
to invade Ukraine,

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:40.410
the vacuum that leaves the
serious situation 10 years on

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:43.560
it's alongside Ukraine,
the biggest refugee crisis.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.690
There are two frozen
conflicts inside Syria now

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:46.890
in the Northwest and the Northeast

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.110
where we have teams of people working,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:50.790
but it's not part of,

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.400
there's no active diplomatic
process to resolve that.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:54.840
And whose fault is that?

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:58.920
Well, I think the vacuum was
created by Western retreat

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.380
and a range of different actors

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:02.910
have moved in to take the place.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.220
A recent policy of the
UK is with the intention

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.770
to deter people from
crossing the English Channel.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.470
You'll be very familiar with
this to enter the UK illegally

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:12.330
has been to deport them to Rwanda.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.580
At the time we're talking no one has been

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.560
because lawyers got involved

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:17.670
and there were human rights issues

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:20.310
and challenges that have
been successful so far.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:22.770
If you look at the polling on it so far,

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:26.130
it is not an insignificant
number of people who support it.

00:14:26.130 --> 00:14:27.480
I'm not saying it's not the
majority who do support it,

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.910
but it's not insignificant.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:33.030
What do you say to those
people who support that,

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:35.820
who think that is the solution for people

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.340
stopping people from making
a very dangerous journey?

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:39.870
Well one they've been told

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:41.790
that the processing of the asylum claim

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.160
will be done in Rwanda, that's not true.

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.560
Your word they're deported
to Rwanda is actually true

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.720
because even if they
pass the asylum claim,

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.790
they have to stay in Rwanda.

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:53.010
Secondly, it's not true to say that

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:55.890
there's no alternative to
deporting people to Rwanda

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:58.440
if you want to manage asylum flows,

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.240
other countries show how
you manage asylum flows

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.370
by running an effective asylum system.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.980
Thirdly, having left the European Union,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:06.270
Britain has lost the right

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.040
to send back to the continent of Europe

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.990
people who failed their asylum claim,

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:12.540
which we had when we were
members of the European Union,

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.460
no British asylum policy can work

00:15:14.460 --> 00:15:16.080
unless it works with France.

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:18.690
It stands to reason because
geography hasn't been ended

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:20.760
by our departure from the European Union.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:22.710
So, there are alternatives to this policy.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.489
That's what I would say to the people

00:15:25.489 --> 00:15:27.660
who are tempted by this policy.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:28.493
You can also find--

00:15:28.493 --> 00:15:30.150
It sounds like you feel like
they've been lied to though?

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.160
Well, there's no question
that they've been,

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.340
it was mis-sold, it was avowedly mis-sold

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:38.040
as external processing of asylum claims

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:40.590
rather than actually the
deportation of asylum seekers

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:41.903
whether or not they pass the test.

00:15:41.903 --> 00:15:45.450
But for those who want something to deter,

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.040
so rather than,

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.450
I know you're talking
about the alternatives,

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:51.090
but they think that something
to stop people getting

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.260
in those dinghies in the first place,

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:53.250
'cause they've reached France,

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:55.620
they've reached safety compared
to where they came from.

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:56.610
Let's address it.

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:57.660
What would you say to that?

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:58.650
I'd say to them,

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:01.440
did you know that the policy says

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:03.330
there will be no deportation

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:04.920
of children and pregnant women?

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:08.430
So there is an incentive
for every people smuggler

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:10.710
to say to a pregnant woman or to a child,

00:16:10.710 --> 00:16:12.090
we'll get you across.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.910
It's incentivizing the people smuggling

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:16.410
of unaccompanied children

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.120
rather than disincentivizing it.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:21.420
And I also can trade polls of 77,

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:23.100
80% of British people who say

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:25.530
that if people have a legitimate reason

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:26.820
to fear going back to their own country,

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.070
they should be given
refuge in this country.

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:30.600
So, in the end it's not about trading--

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:33.150
And also I was just gonna
say of the people who come,

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:35.580
the majority do get the right to stay.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:38.550
Well, that's a very
good point, 70, 80, 90%,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.250
depending whether they're from
Syria or Afghanistan or Yemen

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:42.810
pass the asylum test.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.605
That threshold, even without those roots

00:16:45.605 --> 00:16:46.438
that you are talking about

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:48.960
which could be an alternative
to set up a proper process

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:50.760
so they don't have to come in that way

00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:52.410
or feel that they have to come.

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:53.610
I suppose it's just other countries

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:54.630
are also considering this.

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:56.280
Denmark's looking at it,

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.010
Rwanda are obviously, the
government, not the opposition,

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:01.260
they're saying we're
a great country, come.

00:17:01.260 --> 00:17:02.610
I mean, there's this idea,

00:17:02.610 --> 00:17:05.430
there's an uncomfortableness
around many parts of this,

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:08.040
but why are other countries
also looking at it?

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:09.390
Well, because they're
looking at the numbers

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:10.800
and they're seeing that

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:12.990
there might be some political advantage

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:17.400
in trying to sell the idea
that this is a "deterrent",

00:17:17.400 --> 00:17:19.650
as I've tried to explain,
it's not a deterrent.

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:22.140
So, the deterrent bit
won't work is your point

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:23.460
so we need to do something else.

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:24.510
We know it won't work

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:26.280
'cause we've seen it in other places,

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.220
the push factors that are driving people

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.640
out of countries like
Afghanistan, Yemen, etc,

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.570
are so strong that

00:17:35.850 --> 00:17:38.940
that aspect of the deterrence
is not going to work.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:42.860
What I think does behoove
really serious attention is that

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:46.407
at the moment there isn't a
legal route to claim asylum

00:17:46.407 --> 00:17:48.389
if you are at the moment in Afghanistan.

00:17:48.389 --> 00:17:50.760
Which I think a lot of people don't know

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.250
as a fact about the UK, for instance,

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:55.300
that they will not necessarily know that.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:57.450
I suppose from your perspective,

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:58.980
is it frustrating

00:17:58.980 --> 00:18:00.960
the lack of awareness and understanding

00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:02.970
people have about displaced people

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:04.366
around the world as well?

00:18:04.366 --> 00:18:07.050
Well, I think that the scenes from Ukraine

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:09.300
for all that they can be criticized

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:12.390
as being giving greater attention

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:14.610
than for refugees from other places.

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:16.740
They've created in everyone
that visceral sense,

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:17.970
it could be me.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:18.840
You pack your bag,

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.670
you say goodbye to your
husband or your brother

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:25.410
or your father who's going
off to fight and you leave,

00:18:25.410 --> 00:18:28.900
that's a fundamental
mind shift for people.

00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:31.080
So Ukraine may have woken
people back up to that.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.150
I think it has woken people up.

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:34.890
We know that from the number
of people who are volunteering

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.600
to have Ukrainians come
and stay in their house.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.190
We know that from donations to charities,

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.670
we know that from the overall
public opinion service.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.440
But what we know also know is that

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.930
if countries don't step up together,

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:47.460
then resentment breeds.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:48.960
People say, well, if
people in Germany said,

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:51.570
well, we've taken a million
Syrians, what did France do?

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:53.580
Not very much by comparison.

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.410
So you've gotta step up together.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.180
Do you wanna get back into politics?

00:18:57.180 --> 00:19:00.120
I'm running a $1.4 billion NGO.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:03.240
We are in the middle of
the largest refugee crisis

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.400
since the Second World War

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:08.430
and I feel genuinely lucky
to be doing this job.

00:19:08.430 --> 00:19:11.070
I was in politics, I'm an ex-politician.

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:12.150
All sorts of things can happen to you

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:13.290
after you leave politics.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:14.123
I've found something

00:19:14.123 --> 00:19:17.100
that allows me to put
my values into action.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.870
I feel inspired by the way in which

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:21.810
we've made solutions, not suffering.

00:19:21.810 --> 00:19:23.700
The calling card of the
International Rescue Committee.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:26.280
We've grown from being a
$400 million organization

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:28.500
to being a $1.4 billion organization.

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:31.410
And we've got to take the
lessons of our solutions

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:33.930
to health and to hunger
and to lack of education

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.430
and take them to a wider audience.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:38.250
I always say to people that

00:19:38.250 --> 00:19:42.060
I don't know what I'm gonna do next,

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:44.760
and that may not be a satisfying answer,

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.380
but it's an honest answer.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:49.110
You will be still a keen
watcher of UK politics.

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:51.213
I'd be remiss if I didn't ask you about,

00:19:51.213 --> 00:19:52.650
are you still a member of the Labor Party,

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.470
the left, Keir Starmer, Sir Keir Starmer,

00:19:55.470 --> 00:19:57.120
the leader of the Labor Party.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:00.720
There are people who are very
frustrated by his leadership

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:03.000
when there have been what
they would say open goals

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.820
on the Prime Minister of
the UK, Boris Johnson,

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:09.000
with issues around him
being fined by the police

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.250
through to other trust issues.

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:12.900
Do you share those frustrations?

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:14.820
Well, I am as a British citizen,

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:16.890
I am much more frustrated

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.690
by the policies of the British government

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:23.370
than I am by the performance
of the Labor opposition.

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.340
The country faces massive challenges,

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:30.600
post austerity, post Brexit, post COVID,

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:32.010
post the war in Ukraine

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:34.440
and all that's done to energy prices.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:38.160
And it's not being given
answers by the right in politics

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:39.570
and it needs to be given some answers

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.480
by the center left in politics.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:44.520
And the judgment in the end has gotta be,

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.380
how is it gonna change the country?

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:49.410
We're 25 years since the
'97 Labor election victory

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:50.710
which I was involved with.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.620
21 years since the 2001,
17 years since 2005,

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.360
the three elections
that we won on the trot,

00:20:57.360 --> 00:20:58.860
we won on the trot by presenting

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:00.360
a clear project to the country

00:21:00.360 --> 00:21:01.590
where agree with it or disagree with it,

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:02.580
you knew what it was about

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.700
and then there were policies to emblemize

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:09.000
and to symbolize the change
that was being promised.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.510
That is the task that Keir
Starmer has said that he has.

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:15.000
Do you think we'll have
a Prime Minister Starmer?

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.551
Yes, I think there's
a real chance of that.

00:21:17.551 --> 00:21:21.780
I think he's got depth and
breadth that is important.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.000
And anyone who tells
you, yeah, it's certain,

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.683
it's 100% chance that,

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:28.000
that's not the real world,

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.100
that's partisanship, not prediction,

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.560
but I think he's a serious person

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.280
with a real understanding of how to lead

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:41.850
and a real sense of where
the country's going wrong

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:43.350
and he's gotta bring that out

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.250
and take it to the British people.

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.350
Has he asked for your advice?

00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:48.210
He's got tons of advice.

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:49.980
Well, your brothers in the Shadow Cabinet!

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:50.813
He's got tons of advice,

00:21:50.813 --> 00:21:52.083
he doesn't need, you know,

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:54.330
I'm sure he is watching this program--

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:56.160
Well, we'll make sure, we'll make sure.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:57.900
No, no, but when people
have been through things

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.190
they can reach out to others

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:00.360
who have been through them.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:01.590
And I was also just wanted to bring up

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:04.680
before our time was over
that you took Hillary Clinton

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.782
for a fish and chip dinner,
I believe, recently.

00:22:06.782 --> 00:22:08.430
More than one chip, I can report.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.193
Good, many chips, does she have vinegar?

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.880
In the great vinegar
versus no vinegar divide,

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:15.930
she took vinegar.

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:17.790
She also took tartar sauce

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:19.170
and she also ate mushy peas

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:21.120
without confusing them with avocado puree

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:24.150
which for those of your
viewers who are followers,

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:25.410
acute followers of British politics

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.447
will know that it's a very significant--

00:22:27.447 --> 00:22:28.823
This was in South Shields.

00:22:28.823 --> 00:22:30.270
In South Shields, my former constituency.

00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:31.770
Your former political home.

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:34.200
And you've taken quite a few world leaders

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.850
and dignitaries around that place

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.350
and you always go to
the same fish and chips?

00:22:37.350 --> 00:22:38.910
Correct, sustainable.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:39.876
Sustainable.

00:22:39.876 --> 00:22:41.940
Colmans Fish &amp; Chips
shop on the Ocean Road.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.410
And so it sounds like she enjoyed it,

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:46.320
but I wonder as two former politicians,

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:49.650
have you discussed life beyond

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.903
and what it's like to be out of it now?

00:22:51.903 --> 00:22:52.950
Yes, yes of course.

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:54.570
And look, she did me a great honor

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.310
in coming to South Shields,

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:57.570
which is an ex-mining,

00:22:57.570 --> 00:23:00.120
ex-ship building constituency
in the Northeast of England,

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.810
where I felt embraced
by the local community

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:07.860
and determined to repay
in all sorts of ways

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:09.750
but one way was continuing the tradition

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:10.980
of this South Shields lecture.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:15.570
And she's got an extraordinary
story to tell really,

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:20.370
after 50 years in public
life of some kind or another.

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:22.523
Does she want you to
get back into politics?

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:26.730
She's not pushing.

00:23:26.730 --> 00:23:28.230
She'd be an interesting agent for you.

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:31.140
Agent, I would never put it
that way, she's a friend.

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:33.090
Well, thank you very
much for talking to us

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:35.370
and thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:37.743
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

